 ## Subtopics
 
 ### MetaNLR Baseline
-MetaNLR is a variant of NLR that wraps the NLR implementation with an outer loop to learn the initialization of the shape and color networks using Reptile [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for few-shot novel view synthesis. Meta-training and meta-testing are performed on the same scenes, and specialization to test scenes uses the same training and testing views at 800x600 resolution [S1, unofficial]. The meta-learning rate is set to $5 \times 10^{-2}$ [S1, unofficial]. It uses the same amount of inner loop steps per meta-iteration as the proposed MetaNLR++ method [S1, unofficial].
+MetaNLR is a variant of NLR that wraps the standard NLR implementation with an outer meta-learning loop using the Reptile algorithm [10]. It learns the initialization of the shape and color networks to enable rapid specialization to new scenes. It is chosen when few-shot adaptation to new scenes is required. It uses the same architecture as NLR++ but performs no shape optimization during the inner loop, optimizing only the feature processing networks. The meta-learning rate is set to $5 \times 10^{-2}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** NLR takes 2.21 seconds per iteration [S1, unofficial]. This is significantly longer than NeRF (0.40 sec/iteration) and IDR (0.2 sec/iteration) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Measured Performance:** The timing results were extrapolated from measured iteration times [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** NLR++/MetaNLR++ iterations take longer (2.19 sec/iteration without shape optimization, 7.69 sec/iteration with shape optimization) but require significantly fewer iterations to converge because they optimize the representation for multiple entire images instead of randomly selected rays [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Provides a baseline for comparing meta-learning approaches in neural lumigraph representations.
-- **Limitations:** Slower iteration time compared to some other methods like NeRF and IDR [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When a baseline for NLR is needed, or when comparing against meta-learning variants like MetaNLR.
-- **When not to use:** When computational efficiency per iteration is the primary concern and fewer iterations to converge is not a significant factor.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The training time is 2.21 seconds per iteration [S1, unofficial]. This is significantly slower than NeRF (0.40 sec/iteration) and IDR (0.2 sec/iteration) but comparable to NLR++ (2.19 sec/iteration without shape optimization) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Rendering Time:** Rendering time is 0.025 seconds per frame [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** MetaNLR/NLR++ iterations take longer because they optimize the representation for multiple entire images rather than randomly selected rays, resulting in larger ray-batch sizes [S1, unofficial]. However, they require significantly fewer iterations to converge [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides a stable baseline for neural radiance fields; MetaNLR variant enables fast adaptation to new scenes.
+*   **Limitations:** High per-iteration training time compared to some alternatives; requires careful tuning of meta-learning hyperparameters for the MetaNLR variant.
+*   **When to use:** Use as a baseline for comparing novel view synthesis methods; use MetaNLR when few-shot learning capabilities are needed.
 
